6106819 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106819 #edb/ref 1061 35741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106819 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ace7e49-8905-52e0-be0e-4dd86bba8c36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106819 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106821 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106821 #edb/ref 1061 35801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106821 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c91fbda-be5c-560d-bc46-59441d609e42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106821 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107022 #edb/ref 1064 "The area bounded by a polar curve $r = r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta = \\theta_1$ and $\\theta = \\theta_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2\\,\\textrm d \\theta \\, .\n$$\n\nCalculating the integral is often straightforward if we know the integration limits $\\theta_1$ and $\\theta_2$. But what if we don't know these values?\n\nFor example, suppose we want to calculate the total area of the first petal of the curve $r=\\sin(3\\theta),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0c9059613162af33791e7edb7413dfbac9739cf24eeabd01b0033acba2b772a.png)\n\nThe curve bounding the blue petal crosses itself at the origin. When it passes through the origin, we must have $r=0,$ and since $r=\\sin(3\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(3\\theta)=0 \\, .\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3\\theta = 0,\\pi \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = 0, \\theta_2 = \\dfrac\\pi 3,$ and the area of the first petal is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107022 #edb/ref 1065 1130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107022 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "774c3094-f23a-5a9b-90f7-90e89c6b5883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107022 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107126 #edb/ref 1064 "When working with polar coordinates, we primarily use the following domain for the polar angle $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nHowever, it's sometimes a good idea to use the domain \n\n$$\n-\\pi \\leq \\theta \\lt \\pi.\n$$\n\nChanging the domain for $\\theta$ turns out to be especially useful when a polar petal spans the first and fourth quadrants. \n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107126 #edb/ref 1065 5842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107126 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "529233d0-8f59-53b6-8be1-042c5b5ac84b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107126 #edb/ref 1067 "Using an Alternative Range for the Polar Angle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571532 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186572203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571532 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186572220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571532 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186572222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571532 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186572234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571532 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186572275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571532 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571532 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding the Limits of Integration For a Given Polar Region" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571532 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186572234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571532 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571532 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571532 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b28ca23b-b7d2-5ff8-970b-02d9b3e42bd7" #edb/ref 17592