e8cca29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572137 #edb/ref 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =0 \\\\ 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta$ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $2\\theta$ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n2\\theta =-\\pi ,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the fourth and first quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572141 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186572018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572141 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572141 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572141 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2f/2f0cf87d50bd337546439686d1962a82339bb232c8d9348fb0d47b95e5d56540.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve\n $r=\\sqrt{|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|},$\n represented by the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572141 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71670aa6-98e2-5e7e-99d4-fd373ece53fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572141 #edb/ref 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|}$, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|\\,\\text{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta$ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,2\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the fourth and first quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})|\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572154 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186572081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572154 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572154 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572154 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4d/4dc11bf7a1ee8e7758f1d135bc3ceb6c211cef99de03366152674df7e3de2229.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572154 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92734196-628e-52ff-b0e1-33dc057e48fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572154 #edb/ref 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $8\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n8\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{16},\\frac{\\pi }{16}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{16},\\frac{\\pi }{16}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /16}^{\\pi /16}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572188 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186571997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572188 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127801" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572188 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572188 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/25/25adb6adacb639c284f7607874545f0b13606fed41aedae45ef14623e1d9194c.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572188 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "def58184-cdb7-5356-bdf0-9ddf780782d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572188 #edb/ref 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $10\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n10\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{20},\\frac{\\pi }{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{20},\\frac{\\pi }{20}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /20}^{\\pi /20}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572203 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186572220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572203 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "068550d2-fd3b-5369-9bff-62a34182a03b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572203 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186572141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572220 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186572275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572220 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "31d633ee-4b0e-5a25-9e8f-f0951e5188f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572220 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186572154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572222 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186572203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572222 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c615535-e668-55b4-be46-9132dd27c3b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572222 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186106147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572234 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186572222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572234 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ca3d71f-be4c-543c-ab3a-f955eea47a15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572234 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186572137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572275 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f51cc817-b280-59dc-b7fa-f68667fb902e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186572275 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186572188 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
